WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
Marcus wants to save part of his earnings in a traditional

2
00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.600
individual retirement arrangement, usually called an I R A.

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.840
It can be established through an eligible financial institution, including an

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.980
insurance company. The account is individually owned, even when spouses use

5
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.160
a joint tax return.

6
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.580
Eligibility for a regular contribution generally requires qualifying

7
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.780
compensation, such as wages or net self-employment income.

8
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.920
A pension check or investment interest is not automatically that

9
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.400
compensation. There is no upper age cutoff for regular contributions under

10
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.120
current law, but the other requirements remain.

11
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.440
A spousal contribution can be possible on a joint return using

12
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.740
qualifying combined compensation; it still goes into a separate individual

13
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.942
arrangement. For tax year twenty twenty-six, the general combined limit

14
00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:55.686
for regular traditional and Roth I R A contributions is seven

15
00:00:55.686 --> 00:00:59.922
thousand five hundred dollars, or qualifying compensation if that is less.

16
00:01:00.522 --> 00:01:03.232
Someone age fifty or older can have an additional eleven hundred

17
00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:06.942
dollar catch-up, making the general dollar limit eighty-six hundred dollars.

18
00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:11.322
Suppose Marcus is forty-five and has only four thousand dollars of

19
00:01:11.322 --> 00:01:14.022
qualifying compensation, with no spousal rule.

20
00:01:14.022 --> 00:01:16.682
His regular contribution limit is four thousand dollars.

21
00:01:17.322 --> 00:01:20.602
If he has enough compensation and contributes three thousand dollars to

22
00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:24.262
a traditional account, that generally leaves forty-five hundred dollars of

23
00:01:24.262 --> 00:01:27.742
the combined annual limit, before checking Roth income restrictions.

24
00:01:28.449 --> 00:01:32.569
A traditional I R A contribution may be fully deductible, partly

25
00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:37.629
deductible or nondeductible. Workplace retirement coverage, filing

26
00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:42.349
status and modified adjusted gross income affect the deduction rules.

27
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.069
Being covered by an employer plan does not by itself prohibit

28
00:01:46.069 --> 00:01:47.829
a regular traditional contribution.

29
00:01:48.769 --> 00:01:50.509
It can instead limit the deduction.

30
00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:54.729
If neither spouse is covered by a workplace plan, those income

31
00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:58.649
phaseouts for deducting contributions do not apply, although contribution

32
00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:03.929
limits still do. A permitted nondeductible contribution must be tracked as

33
00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:06.829
basis, generally using Form eighty-six oh six.

34
00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:11.609
Keep that record so after-tax money is not taxed again.

35
00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:16.069
Contribution eligibility and deduction eligibility are separate exam

36
00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:21.035
questions. Growth in a traditional I R A generally is not

37
00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:23.855
taxed annually while it stays inside the arrangement.

38
00:02:24.795 --> 00:02:29.295
If it contains only deductible contributions and untaxed earnings, an

39
00:02:29.295 --> 00:02:32.675
ordinary distribution generally is fully taxable income.

40
00:02:33.495 --> 00:02:37.495
Buying an annuity inside that account does not create an after-tax

41
00:02:37.495 --> 00:02:39.015
investment that never existed.

42
00:02:39.915 --> 00:02:44.115
If nondeductible amounts are present, the required allocation can make

43
00:02:44.115 --> 00:02:48.715
distributions partly taxable; the owner cannot simply choose to withdraw only

44
00:02:48.715 --> 00:02:50.075
basis from one account.

45
00:02:51.075 --> 00:02:55.215
A taxable six thousand dollar distribution at age forty-five, with no

46
00:02:55.215 --> 00:02:59.555
exception, generally also produces six hundred dollars of additional federal

47
00:02:59.555 --> 00:03:04.575
tax. An exception to that additional tax does not automatically remove

48
00:03:04.575 --> 00:03:09.995
ordinary income tax. Consider an owner who turns seventy-three during twenty

49
00:03:09.995 --> 00:03:14.985
twenty-six. The first traditional I R A required minimum distribution is

50
00:03:14.985 --> 00:03:18.645
for that year and generally must be taken by April first

51
00:03:18.645 --> 00:03:22.845
of twenty twenty-seven. The next year's required distribution is due by

52
00:03:22.845 --> 00:03:26.985
December thirty-first of twenty twenty-seven, so delaying the first payment

53
00:03:26.985 --> 00:03:29.265
can put both in one tax year.

54
00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:33.765
Continuing to work does not postpone this traditional account requirement.

55
00:03:33.765 --> 00:03:38.885
Use the applicable birth-year rule rather than memorizing age seventy and

56
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:40.405
one half from old material.

57
00:03:41.165 --> 00:03:45.125
Current law also schedules age seventy-five for younger cohorts.

58
00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:50.585
A required minimum distribution is a minimum withdrawal requirement, not an

59
00:03:50.585 --> 00:03:54.805
instruction to surrender every annuity or spend the entire account.

60
00:03:55.527 --> 00:03:59.747
A surviving spouse can have choices that a nonspouse beneficiary does

61
00:03:59.747 --> 00:04:04.947
not, including an eligible election to treat an inherited account

62
00:04:04.947 --> 00:04:10.307
as their own. Many adult nonspouse designated beneficiaries instead face

63
00:04:10.307 --> 00:04:12.047
a ten-year distribution deadline.

64
00:04:12.927 --> 00:04:16.827
Do not assume that means no earlier annual payments: the owner's

65
00:04:16.827 --> 00:04:20.207
required beginning date and beneficiary category matter.

66
00:04:20.707 --> 00:04:25.307
For an owner dying after the required beginning date, annual beneficiary

67
00:04:25.307 --> 00:04:29.647
distributions generally continue as well as the applicable tenth-year

68
00:04:29.647 --> 00:04:34.867
deadline. A beneficiary's receipt of traditional account earnings is not

69
00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:37.607
automatically excluded life insurance proceeds.

70
00:04:38.427 --> 00:04:43.427
Identify the owner's death date, remaining basis, beneficiary relationship

71
00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:47.267
and applicable category before selecting a payout rule.


